  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining derivative formulas through geometric reasoning. Start with f(x) = x² visualized as the area of a square with side length x. Show that increasing x by dx adds two rectangles of area x·dx and a tiny square of area dx², so df/dx = 2x. Then extend to f(x) = x³ visualized as the volume of a cube with side length x, showing that the dominant change comes from three faces of area x²·dx, giving df/dx = 3x². Generalize to the power rule: derivative of xⁿ is n·x^(n-1). Then tackle f(x) = 1/x using a rectangle of area 1, showing the geometric reasoning for its derivative. Finally, explain the derivative of sin(θ) using the unit circle and similar triangles, showing that d(sin θ)/dθ = cos θ.",
